# The model registry — pipe-separated, one row per servable model.
#
# NAME    the directory under models/ holding the weights, and the name every surface uses
# REPO    HuggingFace MLX repo the weights came from (provenance; nothing here ever pulls)
# DRAFT   MTP draft repo for speculative decoding, or "-" for none
# PORT    default server port — distinct per model so several could run side by side. 8080 is
#         deliberately unused: Docker Desktop binds it (http-alt) for as long as it runs, so a row
#         claiming it fails PortUnavailable on any machine with Docker up.
# SIZE    upstream weight size in GB (du reports GiB, so it reads ~7% smaller)
# FLAGS   this model's best-quality serving flags, space-separated, or "-"
# PARAMS  request-body fields sent with every generation as key=value, space-separated, or "-".
#         Values are JSON, so enable_thinking=false is the boolean and not the string. Separate
#         from FLAGS because a chat template that defaults a behaviour ON cannot be talked out of
#         it by a server flag: the flag's absence leaves the template variable UNDEFINED, which is
#         not false. The request body is the only lever that reaches such a template.
# NOTE    what this one is for, and measured behaviour worth not re-deriving
#
# A row is a claim about what is available UPSTREAM; only the store proves what is on disk.
# Adding a row does not pull anything — downloads stay explicit and user-initiated.
# NOTE must not contain "|": rows are split positionally and a stray pipe is refused by name.
#
NAME|REPO|DRAFT|PORT|SIZE|FLAGS|PARAMS|NOTE
Gemma4-26B-A4B|lmstudio-community/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-MLX-6bit|-|8083|21.8G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false|MoE, ~4B active/token: 61 tok/s decode, 2070 tok/s prefill - 5x the rest on both. Vision + audio, 262K ctx. It DOES think by default and the server flag cannot stop it, so the switch has to be in the request: scripts/probe_thinking_channel.py measured 451 reasoning characters deliberating 'reply with exactly the word: ok' and 1060 on a one-line word problem, against 0 under enable_thinking=false on both. Reasoning scales with task difficulty, so a real benchmark case wastes far more than either probe shows - which is the likeliest source of its 41,751-token cost, the most expensive 7 of 7 in the sweep. thinking_budget is deliberately NOT set here: measured inert on this template (451 and 1060 unchanged at a budget of 16), because the cap watches for delimiters Gemma does not emit, and a cell that does nothing reads as a working control.
Qwen3.8-27B|lmstudio-community/Qwen3.8-27B-MLX-6bit|lukaskremla/Qwen3.8-27B-MTP-6bit-MLX|8089|22.8G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false|Reasoning + agentic coding flagship. Dense, so 24 tok/s with MTP and 427 tok/s prefill; 12.5 tok/s measured here without the draft. Vision, 262K ctx. The MTP draft weights are NOT in the store - pull them before relying on speculative decoding, and note that pulling them permanently forecloses thinking_budget on this row: mlx_vlm raises on that pair, and ResolvedModel now refuses it at resolution rather than at the first generation. That is why the abliterated row carries the hard budget and this one does not - the two levers are mutually exclusive and this row is the one that can buy decode rate. Thinking is switched off in the REQUEST, never by dropping the server flag: with no preference expressed the template variable is left undefined and Qwen3.8's own template reads undefined as on, so thinking survives the flag's absence. It has to be off because thinking-mode output arrives UNFENCED and the edit applier needs a fenced whole-file reply - measured 342 reasoning chars and bare code with the field omitted, 0 and fenced code with it false, and 0 of 7 benchmark cases before it was set against 6 of 7 after, in a seventh of the wall-clock. The last case, 04_auth_service, fell to the corrective re-ask (D-LOOP-006) rather than to any further tuning: it opens with tool_call blocks asking to Read four files, having taken itself for an agent with tools rather than a model given every file it may see, and produces a real file once that reply is quoted back to it. 7 of 7 since, in 3 attempts on that case.
Qwen3.8-27B-abliterated|ailexleon/Huihui-Qwen3.8-27B-abliterated-mlx-6Bit|-|8081|22.8G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false thinking_budget=256|Huihui abliteration of the above - refusal directions removed. No MTP draft, so 12 tok/s. Vision, 262K ctx. It also carries thinking_budget=256 as the HARD half of that switch: enable_thinking is a soft request resolved against the chat template, so the budget bounds a run where the model ignores it - a measured failure mode, one model having leaked 86,469 characters of reasoning on a no-think call. It never fires while the soft request is honoured (0 thinking tokens), and it is safe here only because this row declares no draft: mlx_vlm raises outright on thinking_budget under speculative decoding, which is why the row above cannot carry it and why ResolvedModel refuses the pair rather than letting it fault at generation time. Measured with scripts/probe_thinking_channel.py: 126 reasoning chars with no preference, 0 with enable_thinking=false, and 81 truncated mid-sentence at thinking_budget=16 - the cap works on this <think>-delimited template, unlike Gemma's or harmony's. Same chat template as the row above, so it needs the same in-request thinking switch for the same reason: 0 of 7 benchmark cases before it was set, 5 of 7 after, and 7 of 7 once the corrective re-ask landed (D-LOOP-006). The two it used to miss, 02_schemas and 04_auth_service, both opened with the same tool_call reply as its unabliterated twin and both recovered on the first re-ask.
Gemma4-31B|lmstudio-community/gemma-4-31B-it-MLX-6bit|-|8082|26.1G|--enable-thinking --kv-bits 8 --quantized-kv-start 8192|-|Google dense generalist, the slowest here at 7 tok/s. 140+ languages, strong math. 8-bit KV: fp16 needs 43.8G at 262K and will not fit. Carries --enable-thinking but needs no PARAMS thinking switch, and that is measured rather than assumed: scripts/probe_thinking_channel.py reports 0 reasoning characters across both prompts and all four controls, so its template emits no reasoning channel and the flag is decorative here. Do not copy the sibling MoE row's enable_thinking=false onto it - the cell would be inert and would read as a working control. Its 23,552-token benchmark cost is decode spent on the answer, not on hidden deliberation.
Qwen3-Coder-30B|lmstudio-community/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-MLX-6bit|-|8084|24.8G|-|-|MoE, 3.3B active/token so fast decode like Gemma-MoE. Instruct (no thinking channel). Qwen3-Coder flagship coder, 256K ctx.
GLM-4.7-Flash|lmstudio-community/GLM-4.7-Flash-MLX-6bit|-|8085|24.0G|-|-|MoE ~30B, Zhipu GLM coder-generalist. Instruct. Measured 38.1 tok/s decode, and 2 of 7 benchmark cases - the lowest scorer that still produced code, with two cases emitting Python that does not parse at all.
Qwen3-Coder-Next-4bit|lmstudio-community/Qwen3-Coder-Next-MLX-4bit|-|8087|44.9G|-|-|4bit, so it breaks the 6bit parity of the rest - a win despite lower fidelity is strong, a loss is confounded. qwen3_next hybrid MoE, 80B total / ~3B active (10 of 512 experts) so fast decode. Served and benchmarked end to end on mlx-vlm 0.6.17 at ~46 tok/s decode, loading in under 2 min; it fits 64G alongside a normal desktop, at 19% free memory. 256K ctx.
Muse-Glimmer-30B-6bit|mlx-community/Muse-Glimmer-30B-6bit|-|8086|26.4G|-|-|DENSE 30B, and it decodes like one: 11.5 tok/s, near Gemma4-31B rather than the MoE coders. muse_glimmer vision-language architecture, present in mlx-vlm 0.6.17's model set (checked against the installed package, not assumed). 52 layers, GQA with 2 KV heads, 2048 sliding window, silu, logit softcapping. Instruct, and the config ships do_sample false. 128K ctx. Scores 7 of 7 benchmark cases - the slowest perfect scorer at 38 min, and the untidiest at 49 style findings. This row previously called the model UNUSABLE on the strength of 1 content token in 338s and a bare probe returning 0 in 180s, and blamed harmony-style channel markup; that was the loop misreading a cold weight load as a hang, not the model or its channels, and the reading did not survive the guard being fixed.
gpt-oss-20b|mlx-community/gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4-Q8|-|8088|12.1G|-|-|OpenAI open-weight, the smallest and fastest to load here. gpt_oss MoE 20.9B total / ~3.6B active (4 of 32 experts). NATIVE MXFP4 (4bit experts + 8bit attn = the Q8) - the reference build, not a downgrade, but a distinct quant tier from the 6bit rows. REASONING model, and NO mechanism suppresses it - measured, not assumed. Its analysis channel never reaches reasoning_content (0 chars); it leaks INLINE into content as raw harmony channel markup - the analysis channel, its message, and the switch to the final channel, all as literal special tokens in the content stream (not reproduced here: they contain the delimiter this file is split on). All four of mlx_vlm's controls are inert against it: no preference, enable_thinking=false, and thinking_budget=16 all returned an identical 183 chars, and reasoning_effort=none only shortened it to 164 with the channel still inline. The cause is structural - enable_thinking toggles a chat-template variable and thinking_budget watches <think>/</think>, and harmony has neither - so this is not a weak request that better prompting fixes. It is the likeliest cause of its three repetition derails in the final bench: the edit applier is handed an essay in channel markup instead of a fenced file. Serve it only where that markup is acceptable. Re-run scripts/probe_thinking_channel.py after an mlx_vlm upgrade. 128K ctx.
Qwen3.8-27B-heretic|mlasli/Qwen3.8-27B-Heretic-Abliterated-MLX-6bit|-|8090|21.5G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false thinking_budget=256|Heretic abliteration of the same base as the Huihui row, at the same 6-bit group-64 affine quantization measured from its config.json - so ablation METHOD is the only variable between them. Heretic searches for the refusal directions automatically rather than applying a hand-picked set, which is the claim this row exists to test against Huihui on the seven oracles. No MTP draft, which is what makes the hard budget safe here: mlx_vlm raises on thinking_budget under speculative decoding. Probed with scripts/probe_thinking_channel.py rather than copied from the Huihui row, and it answers the same way on both counts: 110 reasoning chars on a trivial prompt and 393 on a reasoning-inviting one with no preference expressed, 0 on both under enable_thinking=false, and 77 and 81 truncated mid-sentence at thinking_budget=16 - so the soft switch is honoured AND the cap bites on this think-delimited template, which is what a hard half has to do to be worth carrying.
Qwen3.8-27B-abliterated-mtp|KostkaIT/Qwen3.8-27B-Huihui-Abliterated-oQ6e-MTP-MLX|-|8091|23.6G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false|The SAME Huihui abliteration as the 8081 row, republished with multi-token-prediction layers carried INSIDE the main weights - text_config.mtp_num_hidden_layers is present and there is no separate drafter directory, so DRAFT stays - and nothing extra is pulled. That placement matters: the thinking-budget guard keys on a resolved draft PATH, so it cannot fire here, and a thinking budget on this row would reach a speculative server unchecked. Do not give it one. The probe makes that trap concrete rather than theoretical: measured 170 reasoning chars trivial and 1022 reasoning-inviting with no preference, 0 on both under enable_thinking=false, and 74 and 75 truncated at thinking_budget=16 - so the cap LOOKS available and would be set by anyone reading the numbers alone. It is withheld because the danger is not whether the cap works in a probe but that nothing would check it against the speculative path at serve time. The soft switch alone is what this row carries. Its oQ6e quantization is 6-bit group-64 affine with selected attention and MLP layers held at 8 bits.
Gemma4-31B-abliterated|cs2764/gemma-4-31b-it-6bit-abliterated-mlx|-|8092|26.9G|--enable-thinking --kv-bits 8 --quantized-kv-start 8192|enable_thinking=false|Abliterated twin of the 8082 Gemma row at the identical 6-bit group-64 affine quantization, so it isolates ablation on a dense non-Qwen architecture. Carries the same 8-bit KV flags for the same reason as its twin: fp16 KV needs 43.8G at full context and will not fit. The refusal to copy its twin's PARAMS is the reason this row is configured correctly: the twin measures ZERO reasoning characters across both prompts and all four controls, and THIS model measures 278 trivial and 851 reasoning-inviting with no preference expressed. Ablation changed the thinking behaviour, so the twin's blank PARAMS cell would have left it deliberating on every case at full cost. enable_thinking=false takes both to 0. thinking_budget is deliberately NOT set: at a budget of 16 both prompts returned an unchanged 278 and 851, the same inert result its MoE cousin shows, because the cap watches for delimiters Gemma does not emit. Note this is inert for a different reason than the obliterated row is unproven - here reasoning far exceeded the cap and was not truncated, which is a real measurement of an ineffective control.
Qwen3.8-27B-obliterated|OBLITERATUS/Qwen3.8-27B-OBLITERATED|-|8093|22.8G|--enable-thinking|enable_thinking=false thinking_budget=256|OBLITERATUS ablation, quantized HERE rather than pulled ready-made: that repo publishes no MLX quant at all - its config.json carries no quantization key and its only 6-bit artifact is a llama.cpp GGUF the server cannot load. The recipe was copied from the 8081 leader's own config.json, 6 bits group 64 affine, so the comparison is not confounded by quantization; mlx_vlm reported 6.661 effective bits per weight. Only the indexed shard set was fetched - the upstream repo holds a second orphan safetensors set and seven GGUF quants totalling roughly 240G, of which 55.6G is the real model. Probed like every other row: 56 reasoning chars trivial and 2 reasoning-inviting with no preference, 0 on both under enable_thinking=false. It barely deliberates at all compared with its Huihui and Heretic cousins, which measure 393 and 1022 on that same second prompt. thinking_budget=256 rides as the hard half on the strength of the shared think-delimited template, where the cap is measured to truncate on three sibling rows, and NOT on this probe: at a budget of 16 both prompts came back unchanged at 56 and 2, which proves nothing either way because 56 characters was already under the cap. An unbound control is untested, not inert - and the budget earns its place precisely in the case the probe could not reach, a run where the soft switch is ignored.
